<section>
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div class="pic"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1758685849121-c24d74e8f775?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="excited teacher celebrating in front of a blackboard" width="800" height="672" loading="lazy"></div>
    <div>
      <span class="cr">Feedback that works</span>
      <h2>Turning assessment into progress</h2>
      <p>Assessment is most useful when it helps learners take the next step. Marks alone rarely do that; specific, timely feedback usually does.</p>
      <ul class="gems"><li>Share success criteria before the task begins.</li><li>Give feedback while there&#8217;s still time to act on it.</li><li>Focus on one or two improvements, not ten.</li><li>Build in time for learners to respond and redraft.</li><li>Use rubrics so learners can assess their own work.</li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap faq"><span class="cr">Questions</span><h2 style="margin-bottom:20px">Pedagogy FAQ</h2><details open><summary>What is pedagogy?</summary><p>Pedagogy is the method and practice of teaching: how teachers plan, explain, question, assess and support learners. It draws on both research about how people learn and the professional judgement of teachers.</p></details><details><summary>What is Bloom&#8217;s taxonomy?</summary><p>Bloom&#8217;s taxonomy is a framework that sorts learning goals by complexity, from remembering facts to creating something new. The revised version from 2001 uses six levels: remember, understand, apply, analyse, evaluate and create.</p></details><details><summary>What&#8217;s the difference between formative and summative assessment?</summary><p>Formative assessment happens during learning to guide the next steps, like a quick quiz or exit ticket. Summative assessment measures learning at the end of a unit, like a final test or project.</p></details><details><summary>Why use a rubric?</summary><p>A rubric makes expectations clear before learners start, helps teachers mark consistently and gives learners specific feedback about what to improve.</p></details><details><summary>Which learning theory is best?</summary><p>No single theory explains all learning. Effective teachers draw on several: clear explanations and practice from cognitive approaches, active exploration from constructivism and support from peers from social learning.</p></details></div></section>

